aa2-301d-5486-9223-105546674dc7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134597 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134598 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134598 #edb/ref 1061 151856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134598 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fd815b69-da3f-5d4e-b49a-e75f1d8f0398" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134598 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134599 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134599 #edb/ref 1061 151857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134599 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "79b94128-fada-50aa-8ec5-3f3315b85c77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134599 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134600 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134600 #edb/ref 1061 151861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134600 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "266a9ffe-5ee6-5f21-95a7-a8aea3b7a60d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134600 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134601 #edb/ref 1061 151864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134601 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2fd6a3e8-b2b4-5760-a3c1-067f79a80a5b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134601 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135183 #edb/ref 1064 "To **cube** a number means to multiply that number by itself and then multiply the result by the original number once more.\n\nFor example, to \"cube $2$\", we need to multiply $2$ by $2$ to make $4,$ and then multiply this result by $2$ once more. We write the \"cube of $2$\" as $2^3,$ so we have\n\n$$\n2^3 = \\underbrace{2\\cdot 2}_{\\large 4} \\cdot 2 = 4 \\cdot 2 = 8.\n$$\n\nWe would then say that \"the cube of $2$ is $8$\". We could also say \"$2$ cubed equals $8$\".\n\nThe number ${\\color{blue}3}$ in the expression $2^{\\color{blue}3}$ is called the power (or exponent).\n\nThe act of cubing a number can be represented geometrically. For example, let's start by representing the number $2$ with the following collection of two items:\n\n![](images/b6/b6a13017dcbcf25a66ab576189690e8879a4ccbb3c4c5562b129f4dd15af4e85.png)\n\nThen we can picture $2^3$ by creating a bigger shape (called a **cube**) with length, width and height all equal to $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/30/305e02bdaa4c3efcb9d8047eeb3d3573f39c08714e30ba6053cfc4441053156b.png)\n\nThere are $8$ items in total in our big cube (one little cube is hiding behind the others), indicating that $2^3=8.$\n\nClearly, $1^3=1\\cdot1\\cdot1={\\color{blue}1}.$ The next few first basic cubes are shown below:\n\n| $2^3=2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2={\\color{blue}8}$ | $3^3=3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3={\\color{blue}27}$ | $4^3=4 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 4={\\color{blue}64}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ![](images/30/305e02bdaa4c3efcb9d8047eeb3d3573f39c08714e30ba6053cfc4441053156b.png) | ![](images/cb/cb3171dc7f7a2e9838c3e8b46c3ce385be28624b4474391b9bb211b5a599fa55.png) | ![](images/65/6546ffc5df407b29df601252e1f5afa8e01b17dca5c7169a6321d550d86255c3.png) |\n\nThe cubes of our usual counting numbers are called **cube numbers** or **perfect cubes**. We can write them as follows:\n\n$$\n1^3, 2^3, 3^3, 4^3, 5^3, 6^3, \\ldots \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad {\\color{blue}1}, {\\color{blue}8}, {\\color{blue}27}, {\\color{blue}64}, {\\color{blue}125}, {\\color{blue}216}, \\ldots\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135183 #edb/ref 1065 8272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135183 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1bd95aec-179b-5dc4-b7bd-da02ca5932f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135183 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135184 #edb/ref 1064 "Cubing a negative number *always* results in another negative number.\n\nThis is because a negative times a negative gives a positive value, and then a positive times a negative is a negative again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-)\\,\\cdot \\,(-)\\,\\cdot \\,(-)\\,=\\,{{(-)\\,\\cdot \\,(-)}_{⏟}}_{(+)}\\,\\cdot \\,(-)\\,=\\,(+)\\,\\cdot \\,(-)\\,=\\,(-)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}-5})^3 = \\underbrace{({\\color{red}-5}) \\cdot ({\\color{red}-5})}_{\\large {\\color{blue}25}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}-5}) = {\\color{blue}25} \\cdot ({\\color{red}-5}) = {\\color{red}-125}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135184 #edb/ref 1065 8273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135184 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e462ead2-4e55-5dac-a940-22768a77546e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135184 #edb/ref 1067 "The Cube of a Negative Number" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475446 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{15}{8}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475446 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475446 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "015ae1f8-8a9d-59b7-891f-b25c7b09ae9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475453 #edb/ref 1080 "-64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475453 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475453 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "04d60ba7-e634-59ed-b05f-ef7c895f87d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475457 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{64}{125}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475457 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475457 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "07991899-634d-5f88-87f5-568a30dd3254" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475467 #edb/ref 1080 "-729" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475467 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475467 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "108b4415-53eb-5f00-9db9-d4131952781f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475473 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{125}{6}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475473 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475473 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "13a43bea-0643-56a8-b165-17d7e4754f2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475483 #edb/ref 1080 "75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475483 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475483 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1fba4aa5-0f41-512c-aee9-ad567870f839" #e